### Atomic File Replacement Pattern in Rust

Source: https://context7.com/stebalien/tempfile/llms.txt

Provides a complete example of the atomic file replacement pattern, often used for safe configuration updates. It involves creating a temporary file in the same directory as the target, writing new content, syncing to disk, and then atomically renaming it to replace the original file. This ensures that other processes see either the old or the new version, never a partial one.

```rust
use tempfile::NamedTempFile;
use std::io::{Write, Result};
use std::fs::File;

fn update_config_atomically(new_config: &str) -> Result<()> {
    // Create temp file in same directory as target
    // (required for atomic rename on same filesystem)
    let mut tmpfile = NamedTempFile::new_in("/etc/myapp")?;

    // Write new configuration
    write!(tmpfile, "{}", new_config)?;

    // Sync to disk to ensure durability
    tmpfile.as_file().sync_all()?;

    // Atomically replace old config
    // Other processes see either old or new config, never partial
    tmpfile.persist("/etc/myapp/config.toml")?;

    Ok(())
}

fn main() -> Result<()> {
    let config = "[server]\nport = 8080\nhost = \"0.0.0.0\"";

    // In real usage, would write to actual config location
    // update_config_atomically(config)?;

    Ok(())
}
```

