### Persist Named Temporary File Atomically in Rust

Source: https://context7.com/stebalien/tempfile/llms.txt

This Rust function creates a NamedTempFile, writes config data, and atomically persists it to a target path using the persist method, which replaces existing files atomically on Unix. Dependencies are tempfile, std::io, and std::fs. Input is the target path; output is the persisted file. Limitations include atomicity depending on filesystem support.

```rust
use tempfile::NamedTempFile;
use std::io::{Write, Read};
use std::fs::File;

fn main() -> std::io::Result<()> {
    // Create and write to temporary file
    let mut tmpfile = NamedTempFile::new()?;
    writeln!(tmpfile, "Configuration data v2.0")?;
    writeln!(tmpfile, "setting1=value1")?;
    writeln!(tmpfile, "setting2=value2")?;

    // Atomically replace old config file
    // If config.txt exists, it's replaced atomically
    let saved_file = tmpfile.persist("./config.txt")?;

    // Verify the persisted file
    let mut contents = String::new();
    let mut file = File::open("./config.txt")?;
    file.read_to_string(&mut contents)?;
    assert!(contents.contains("setting1=value1"));

    // Cleanup for demo
    std::fs::remove_file("./config.txt")?;
    Ok(())
}
```

